  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a nasty cough, stuffy nose, sinus congestion, coughing up mucus, chest pain when coughing, loss of smell, and muscle aches. These symptoms align almost perfectly with Reference [1], which describes coughing, stuffy nose, facial/sinus pressure, phlegm, chest pain from coughing, inability to smell, and muscle pain, leading to a diagnosis of common cold. While allergies can cause nasal congestion and cough, they typically involve itchy eyes and sneezing rather than prominent muscle aches and acute multi-day viral symptoms. Pneumonia would generally involve shortness of breath, high fever, and significant systemic weakness, which are absent here. Bronchial asthma centers on wheezing and breathing difficulty. Therefore, the clinical facts firmly establish common cold as the single correct diagnosis.",
